X3Fuse modifications to the code (not to the data)
--------------------------------------------------

  - Integration into this crate's single-device, single-submission, tiled pass
    list instead of spektrafilm-rs's whole-frame CPU round trips.
  - Spatial parameters are derived from the whole sensor frame at the current
    render scale, so cropping or requesting a region never changes a diffusion
    size or a grain particle.
  - Grain is anchored to native image coordinates rather than the tile-local
    pixel index, so tiles, regions and whole previews agree.
  - An added grain chroma control mixes the per-channel noise toward its
    achromatic mean.
  - Grain size scales particle area and optical blur. Stock RMS calibration
    uses the fitted sublayer curves. The grain USM uses local mass correction
    to keep tiles and cropped regions consistent; upstream uses one global
    image scalar. The random sequence is specific to this renderer.
  - An added grain roughness control clumps the particles: a smooth random
    field anchored to native pixels varies the particle count and inversely
    the particle density, keeping the mean density.
  - `halation_midtones` gates the added halation by the blurred signal level;
    spektrafilm's separate highlight-boost stage is not ported.
  - The recipe sets spektrafilm's per-channel scatter and halation sizes and
    strengths. Scatter size sets the exponential decay length; the Gaussian
    core keeps its ratio to it. Stock selection sets halation defaults from
    the film use and antihalation tags. Saved channel overrides are preserved.
  - The enlarger is normalised against an uncompensated mid-gray card
    (`normalize_print_exposure` without `print_exposure_compensation`).
    spektrafilm's default re-solves the print exposure at the compensated film
    exposure, which cancels a film exposure change back out and leaves only a
    shift in where the scene sits on the H&D curve; X3Fuse's film exposure
    control has to expose the film and let the print follow it.
  - The scanner white and black correction (`color_reference`) is always on
    for prints and slides. Its references come from this crate's own tables
    (normalized film curves, modelled paper curves), so they match its print.
  - Auto exposure is replaced by camera metering: the film exposes the scene
    value the camera's own tone curve renders as 18% gray as its mid-gray.
  - Blurs whose radius would round away are skipped.
  - f32 preview precision throughout, against spektrafilm's f64 export path.
  - Stages not ported: glare, highlight boost, scanner unsharp masking,
    camera/enlarger diffusion filters, camera and scanner lens blur, alternate
    spectral upsampling methods, development-time choice, LUT export, and
    chemistry controls beyond the existing film and paper contrast sliders.
    B&W stocks use the default middle development time.
  - B&W stocks run through the three-channel pipeline as three identical
    channels. Each carries a third of the silver density, all channels share
    one grain draw, DIR couplers inhibit only the same layer, and per-channel
    constants take the first channel's value, as spektrafilm's
    `match_channels` does. Grain uses the stock RMS preset.
  - A B&W negative is printed without enlarger filtration: its density is the
    same at every wavelength, so the print exposure normalisation cancels any
    filtration.
  - A taking filter is scaled so a neutral subject exposes the film as
    without it, like a meter that reads through the filter. spektrafilm
    leaves the loss to its auto exposure. Filter curves are interpolated
    linearly onto the 5 nm grid where spektrafilm uses Akima.

Runtime dependencies added for rendering/encoding include wgpu (MIT/Apache-2.0),
pollster (MIT/Apache-2.0), bytemuck (Zlib/Apache-2.0/MIT), serde/serde_json
(MIT/Apache-2.0), libm (MIT/Apache-2.0), half (MIT/Apache-2.0), and image
(MIT/Apache-2.0). image supplies PNG and genuine JPEG encoding; TIFF output uses
x3f-core's existing encoder. Their source and license terms remain available
through Cargo.lock and the associated crate distributions.
